course: dictionary
lesson: 11629
title: Chosen without a reason
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Arbitrarily is an adverb, a word that tells you how something is done. It comes from the adjective arbitrary, which means chosen without a good reason, with -ly added to describe the action. «The teacher picked the team arbitrarily, pointing at names on the list with her eyes shut.» Nothing in the sentence shows a reason for the choice, so arbitrarily means without a real reason.
explain: Look for clues around the word. In «The rules were made arbitrarily, and nobody could say why they existed», the words nobody could say why are the clue. Try your meaning in the sentence: «The rules were made without a real reason» still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: In maths, you can choose a number arbitrarily, which means any number will do. In everyday talk, rules made arbitrarily can feel unfair, because nobody can explain them. Look out for the phrase «chosen arbitrarily».
fact: Arbitrarily means without a real reason or plan, or by a choice that is not fair.
fact: «Chosen arbitrarily» means picked with no real reason, often by chance.
fact: In maths, «choose a number arbitrarily» means any number at all will do.
fact: Arbitrary is the adjective; arbitrarily is the adverb that describes how something is done.
fact: Rules made arbitrarily can feel unfair, because nobody can explain why they are there.
exercise: «The class captain was chosen arbitrarily, with no vote and no reason.» What does arbitrarily mean? | *Without a real reason, by chance | Loudly, with a cheer | After a long, careful vote | With great kindness
exercise: In maths, you can pick a number arbitrarily. What does arbitrarily mean here? | It must be a prime number | *Any number will do | Only the biggest number counts | Write it in words
exercise: «The rules were made arbitrarily, and nobody could say why they existed.» What does the phrase nobody could say why tell you? | They were very old | They were written in gold | They were loved by everyone | *They had no good reason
test: «The prizes were given out arbitrarily.» Which phrase means the same? | In alphabetical order | *By chance, with no real reason | Very slowly, one at a time | With a long speech first
test: Priya says arbitrarily means very carefully. Is she right? | No, it means with a smile | Yes, it means very quickly | *No, it means without a real reason or plan | Yes, it means with great care
exam: «Why do rules made arbitrarily often seem unfair?» | Because they make everyone happy | *Because nobody can explain them with a real reason | Because they are always too short | Because they are always written in capital letters
